          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 314,
                "quote": "shelf branchId library |> Result.map (fun view -> ..."
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The change adds no new domain rule. The inventory, holds and members CSVs are projections in Tier 3 (Views and Reports) built on the existing `shelf`, `holdQueue` and `memberTypeName`. Branch validation comes from the existing `shelf` result, and hold queue order comes from `holdQueue`. Sorting hold books by id is output ordering, not a lending rule. The CLI only parses and renders.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 17,
                "quote": "ports.Files.WriteLines path (\"copy,member,due,days overdue\" :: rows)"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "All three new exports write through the existing `ports.Files.WriteLines` port. The change also replaces the direct `File.WriteAllLines` in `exportOverdue` with the port and drops `open System.IO` from Tier 3. No effect is added in Tier 1/2, and the CLI does not call Tier 2 or Persistence. The CSV builders are pure functions that return lines.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "Each fact has one definition that is reused. Member type names come from `memberTypeName`, the same function the member list uses. Hold positions and dates come from `holdQueue`. Loan due dates come from the shelf view. The header strings are each written once. The \"on shelf\" status text appears once, in `inventoryCsv`.",
            "score": 2
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 196,
                "quote": "Catalog.addCopy { Id = CopyId copyId; Book = BookId bookId; Branch = BranchId branch } library"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 328,
                "quote": "match Holds.queueFor (BookId book) library with"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The rules for adding a copy (DEC-011) are applied by Tier 2 `Catalog.addCopy` and `Catalog.findBranch`. The hold queue is read from Tier 2 `Holds.queueFor`. Tier 3 only parses `copy,book` lines, wraps errors with line numbers, and chooses the status label from the queue length. The all-or-nothing behaviour comes from folding over a `Result` inside `commit`. Tier 3 adds no legality checks of its own.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 175,
                "quote": "match ports.Files.ReadLines path with | None -> Error(InvalidInput $\"cannot read {path}\")"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The file is read through the existing `Files` port, and persistence goes through the existing `commit` and `Store` path. No clock, I/O or console is added in Tier 1/2, and the CLI only dispatches to the use case. Hold counting is pure.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 328,
                "quote": "| queue -> $\"{c.Copy},{book},{c.Title},on shelf ({queue.Length} holds waiting)\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The hold count comes from `Holds.queueFor`, so the number of holds on a book has a single source. Branch and copy rules are not restated in the change, and error text reuses `Views.describeError`. The `queueFor` call here and the earlier `holdQueue` view are two routes to the same queue. I found nothing in the diff that makes them diverge.",
            "score": 2
          }
        }
